 are  experiencing who are trying to do this  type of work cost efficiency privacy  concerns turnaround time as I mentioned  too few genetic counselors one reason  that we utilize physicians as kind of  the primary point person to return  results is that there's so many more  physicians than there are genetic  counselors only about 3,000 in this  country genetic counselors so it's about  one for every hundred and thirty  thousand people and so if clinical  sequencing were to become much more  widespread there would be you know a  problem if we felt that genetic  counselors needed to be involved in each  and every case and so in in utilizing  physicians as a primary sort of point of  contact in our study we felt that it  would kind of be more ecologically valid  more to a real world if you will and  then also the issue of physician  training which has been again one of  these hot button issues that's been  widely discussed what you think what you  can see here this is a compilation of  some of the genomic medicine initiatives  in this country up until about spring of  this year I'm sure there are a few more  at this point in time there's about in  the and this is very encouraging a  number of these schools um sort of  developing genomic medicine curriculum  for undergraduates for medical students  and so forth but I think what's what's  important as a 16 initiatives but  there's actually over 140 accredited  medical schools so um you know this  isn't a new issue but certainly the  issue of physician training was sort of  looms large in this space another  that we're pursuing at Scripps to try to  you know further build on some of the  ideas and the results that we've seen an  idiom is actually a partnership with  several primary care in internal  medicine and family medicine physicians  here at Scripps who have signed on to  participate in a study where each of  them will enroll four to six patients  those patients will undergo sequencing  and these physicians have all signed on  to to try to return results to these  patients and further explore that  process and how we can effectively  integrate genome sequencing into the two  medicine turn into primary care so we're  we're looking forward to that initiative  so just to to conclude I want to revisit  kind of the main points that I mentioned  at the beginning of the talk and I think  one of the observations that I'm calling  these observations because this is case  study data this isn't a you know a large  randomized control trial but I think  we're seeing some hints that and  probably doesn't surprise anyone but  physicians as a group kind of need may  need help with genetics they and you  know and in some cases like our case  number three they might not know it so  um really you know kind of further you  know understanding these issues and  trying to figure out how we can increase  support for a physician and training is  obviously very important make another  important point and take home  observation is that in patients families  and physicians um would kind of more  often than not choose to receive some if  not all secondary or incidental findings  so kind of a message that these  individuals want access to their own  data want any and all data and findings  that are available and so thinking about  how to do that um how to  um you know potentially provide patients  with the choice about which ones which  findings they want to receive I think  further exploring that issue with  empirical studies is going to be  critical as I mentioned there's a lot of  commentaries and opinion pieces about  this but better understanding what is  the impact of delivering incidental  findings on the patients on the system  the healthcare system I think is going  to be important going forward and then  finally um just to underscore this point  again is that patients in these early  studies or at least in our early study  this diagnostic Odyssey type study are  not you know and I don't think this is a  surprise to anyone are not your average  patients and you know people who who may  be benefiting from sequencing in these  early stages are people who are kind of  area where are very savvy potentially  have a lot of financial resources so  thinking about how you know how we can  ensure that these services and this  technology is available to to all types  of patients who may benefit I think is  also important and I just want to  acknowledge a many people here at  Scripps who have made these studies  possible there's certainly has taken you  know the involvement for many people in  our group to to get the study off the  ground to to run these patients through  so very very appreciative here so that's  the conclusion of my talk um I'll take  questions now  okay so I have a one question here um  let me just read it an extremely  interesting study a number of  suggestions and hands appeared as far as  I understood five patients were enrolled  starting from approximately 100 eligible  cases you can for some metrics only four  cases were evaluated how did you address  the possible biases link to this low  recruitment rate have you planned  subsequent studies so this is a great  question I think just to you know it  kind of clarify a bit more we the  hundred cases that I that I mentioned in  the beginning we're actually not all  eligible these were all of our referrals  many of them you know did not come even  close to meeting our inclusion criteria  so we basically as I mentioned the  processes that for cases that appear as  though they might meet criteria we to  bring them forward to undergo panel  review by our clinician scientist panel  and then from there those that panel  actually chooses who to enroll and so  we've actually never had an instance  where the panel has voted to enroll a  case but that that family has declined  enrollment so I certainly agree that  that this is a very kind of bias  selected sample and I sort of intended  to elucidate some of 